<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- =============================================================================
     cyclonedds_laptop.xml — WORKSTATION side (RViz2), peer of cyclonedds_gos.xml.

     READ THIS FIRST: a DDS profile is NOT what stands between this laptop and the
     GOS topics. There is no IP path by default, and that gap is BELOW DDS —
     matching ROS_DOMAIN_ID and RMW_IMPLEMENTATION cannot bridge it and no value
     in this file can either.

         ping 10.21.31.104

     must succeed before any DDS debugging is meaningful. Run
     scripts/check_remote_viz.sh, which walks every layer in order and stops at
     the first one that is actually broken.

     TWO WAYS TO GET THAT IP PATH
     ============================
     (A) ROUTED VIA THE AOS over wifi — the multihop path this file is currently
         configured for:

             nav_core @ 10.21.31.104  ==[eth2]==>  AOS  ==[p2p0]==>  laptop @ 192.168.8.x

         WHICH MACHINE NEEDS WHAT — the three roles are NOT interchangeable, and
         every command below is tagged because putting the route on the wrong box
         is the easiest way to break a working link:
           the LAPTOP needs a ROUTE       (it is on neither subnet)
           the AOS    needs FORWARDING    (it is on BOTH; it must never be given
                                           a route to a subnet it already sits on)
           the GOS    needs a ROUTE BACK  (it is on the robot subnet only)

         [AOS]   (10.21.31.103 on eth2 / 192.168.8.103 on p2p0), once:
             sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
             # let laptop-initiated (NEW) traffic into the robot subnet — the
             # stock RELATED,ESTABLISHED rule alone silently drops DDS discovery
             sudo iptables -I FORWARD 1 -i p2p0 -o eth2 -j ACCEPT
             sudo iptables -I FORWARD 1 -i eth2 -o p2p0 -j ACCEPT
             # and do NOT masquerade robot<->laptop traffic: SNAT rewrites the
             # source to 192.168.8.103 while the RTPS locators inside still say
             # 10.21.31.104, which makes every discovery problem unreadable
             sudo iptables -t nat -I POSTROUTING 1 -s 10.21.31.0/24 -d 192.168.8.0/24 -j ACCEPT
             sudo netfilter-persistent save
             # DO NOT add 'ip route ... 10.21.31.0/24 via 192.168.8.103' HERE.
             # eth2 already gives the AOS a connected route to that subnet, and
             # 192.168.8.103 is the AOS's OWN p2p0 address — it points the subnet
             # back at itself and the AOS immediately stops reaching the GOS.

         [LAPTOP] the wifi router at 192.168.8.1 has no route to the robot
                  subnet, so the default route will not do it. THIS is the only
                  machine that needs this command:
             sudo ip route add 10.21.31.0/24 via 192.168.8.103
         With 'network_mode: host' the container shares this namespace, so add it
         on the HOST; there is nothing to add inside the container.
         Then set <Interfaces> below to this laptop's wifi address.

         [GOS]   (10.21.31.104) needs the reply path, or the request arrives and
                 dies there. 'ip route' edits do NOT survive a reboot:
             sudo ip route replace 192.168.8.0/24 via 10.21.31.103 dev eth0
         Persist in /etc/netplan/*.yaml. Prefer this specific route over
         replacing the GOS's default route, which disturbs its other traffic.

     (B) CABLED INTO THE ROBOT'S INTERNAL SWITCH — simpler, and preferred when
         the laptop is physically at the robot. Same L2 broadcast domain as the
         GOS, so multicast SPDP works natively and the <Peers> block below is
         unnecessary:
             sudo ip addr add 10.21.31.50/24 dev <wired-if>   # .103=AOS .104=GOS
             sudo ip link set <wired-if> up
             ping -c3 10.21.31.104
         Then set <Interfaces> to 10.21.31.50 and DELETE the <Peers> block.

     Usage, in the same shell as rviz2:
       export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp    # required; nav_core is Cyclone
       export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0                          # matches the GOS containers
       unset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY                        # =1 pins the RMW to loopback
       export CYCLONEDDS_URI=file://$PWD/config/cyclonedds_laptop.xml
       rviz2 -d custom_nav2.rviz

     BANDWIDTH: custom_nav2.rviz ships with a PointCloud2 display on
     /lidar/points. That is ~1.2 MB x 10 Hz = ~96 Mbit/s, which will not fit this
     wifi hop and starves the nav topics of airtime. UNTICK it (or delete the
     display) for remote viz and watch /scan instead. See check_remote_viz.sh.
     ============================================================================= -->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config"
            xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
            xsi:schemaLocation="https://cdds.io/config https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eclipse-cyclonedds/cyclonedds/master/etc/cyclonedds.xsd">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <!-- MUST name an address that actually exists on this machine. An address
           that matches no interface is not a harmless placeholder — Cyclone
           refuses to start and RViz dies before it reaches the network:
             10.21.34.__: does not match an available interface
             dds_create_participant(domain -1) failed: -1

           Pinning is needed because Cyclone otherwise picks ONE interface and on
           this laptop several candidates tie. Measured with Verbosity=config:
             interfaces: lo udp/127.0.0.1(q1) wlp131s0f0 udp/192.168.8.236(q9)
                         tailscale0 udp/100.105.97.80(q9) docker0 udp/172.17.0.1(q9)
             using network interface wlp131s0f0 (udp/192.168.8.236)
                         selected ARBITRARILY from: wlp131s0f0, tailscale0, docker0
           "Arbitrarily" is Cyclone's own word — an equal-quality tie is not
           stable across reboots or interface reordering, and picking tailscale0
           or docker0 here means silence with no error.

           SET THIS to whichever address on THIS machine reaches the GOS:
             192.168.8.___  wifi, for the routed-via-AOS path (A) — CHECK IT,
                            it is DHCP and has already changed once (.236 -> .160)
             10.21.31.50    once cabled to the internal switch (B)
           Confirm the choice with:  ip route get 10.21.31.104 -->
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface address="192.168.8.160" priority="default" multicast="default"/>
      </Interfaces>

      <!-- Matches the GOS: multicast for participant discovery, unicast for all
           DATA. Do not widen to 'true' — that would put the clouds on multicast.
           On path (A) multicast SPDP cannot cross the AOS hop at all, so
           discovery there rests entirely on <Peers> below. -->
      <AllowMulticast>spdp</AllowMulticast>
    </General>

    <!-- DELETE THIS WHOLE BLOCK on path (B): a shared L2 segment discovers the
         GOS by multicast on its own and peers only add probe traffic.

         KEEP IT on path (A), where multicast cannot cross the router hop.

         THE PEER IS DELIBERATELY PORTLESS. A <Peer> with an explicit port probes
         exactly ONE participant, and SPDP has no relay — each participant
         discovers independently. nav_core plus m20_bridge are ~20 SEPARATE
         PROCESSES (amcl, ekf, map_server, planner, smoother, controller,
         route_server, orchestrator, lifecycle, the restamps, the uds relays...),
         each its own DDS participant on its own index and therefore its own
         port. A peer pinned to one port (e.g. 10.21.31.104:7430, index 10) makes
         RViz discover that single participant and nothing else — which looks
         like a partial, baffling topic list rather than a clean failure.

         Portless instead expands to one SPDP datagram per index in
         0..MaxAutoParticipantIndex, at
             port = 7400 + 250*domain + 10 + 2*index      (domain 0: 7410..7530)
         covering every participant on the host. 61 small datagrams per discovery
         round is a few kB — irrelevant next to the topic data.

         MaxAutoParticipantIndex must stay well above Cyclone's default of 9: the
         robot's own Fast-DDS stack shares this domain and port space and holds
         indices 0-9, so the GOS containers land at index 10 and up. -->
    <Discovery>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="10.21.31.104"/>
      </Peers>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>60</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>

    <Internal>
      <!-- SILENTLY CLAMPED to net.core.rmem_max, measured at 212992 (208 KB) on
           this laptop — far too small for a ~1.2 MB PointCloud2, while small
           topics like /imu and /tf look perfectly healthy. Raise it here too:
             sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=16777216
           Persist in /etc/sysctl.d/ to survive reboot. -->
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="8MB"/>
    </Internal>

    <Tracing>
      <!-- Set to 'config' and re-run to see which interface was actually
           selected and which peers were reached. First thing to check when RViz
           shows no topics. Note the trace comes from the ros2 daemon, not the
           CLI process, so point OutputFile at a file rather than stderr. -->
      <Verbosity>warning</Verbosity>
      <OutputFile>stderr</OutputFile>
    </Tracing>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
